  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nA mid-level closed low is currently positioned over the eastern\nCONUS with an associated surface low centered in western PA. In\nthe local area, weak surface high pressure is centered over\neastern KS leading to light northwesterly surface winds across\nthe CWA. Despite some lingering cloud cover from the system to\nthe east, these northerly winds along with CAA at 850-hPa will \ncontinue to reinforce cooler temperatures through the morning. \nLow temperatures are expected to drop into the mid-to-upper \n20s.\n\nA mid-level ridge axis moves overhead today bringing subsidence\nand clearing skies from west to east. A surface ridge will also\nshift eastward, turning winds to a southerly direction by the \nafternoon. Mid-level height rises and WAA will combine to allow\nhigh temperatures to climb into the upper 40s and lower 50s. \nCloud cover returns this evening as the mid-level ridge shifts \noff to the east and a weak shortwave moves overhead. Insulation\nform the cloud cover should help low temperatures remain just \nabove freezing tonight. Saturday will see primarily zonal flow \naloft, leading to a similar forecast as today, with highs in the\nlow-to-mid 50s and light winds (<5 mph).\n\nThe next chance for precipitation comes Saturday evening into\nSunday. A mid-level shortwave will eject off the central Rockies\nSaturday afternoon, inciting lee cyclogenesis in the vicinity of\nthe OK panhandle. Current expectation is for the surface low to\ntrack through northern AR and southern MO early Sunday morning.\nThis will position the local area on the northern side of the \nstorm with ample moisture wrapping around the cyclone creating a\nsaturated profile through the full depth of the troposphere. \nThe good news (assuming you don't enjoy winter weather) is that \ntemperatures are expected to remain well above freezing through\nthe duration of the overnight period (lows in the mid-to-upper\n30s). However, it will be a soggy night with rain chances of\n80-95% across the whole CWA between 00Z and 12Z on Sunday.\nEnsemble guidance depicts some discrepancy on where the highest\nrainfall accumulations will be, but expectation is for \nwidespread totals of at least 0.25\", with the best chances \nfor this total to be exceed (60-80% chance) being along and \nnorth of the Missouri River.\n\nOnce this system moves off to the east, zonal mid-level flow is\nexpected to return through the first half of the work week. \nSurface high pressure builds in to the south and east of the\nlocal area, turning winds back to southerly. Temperatures are \nforecast to be well above normal with highs pushing 60F by \nmidweek. Model guidance depicts another trough and surface \ncyclone ejecting over the central CONUS in the back half of next\nweek, although, confidence is low in the timing and placement \nof this system. However, this looks like the next chance for \nsome precipitation across the lower Missouri River Valley.",
